2186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087729 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Intercepts of Ellipses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186087921 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "738132c2-94a1-5f53-8c93-d33a4d0655d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93774ab1-c034-5d25-91bb-1dbd3d1f509d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088044 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=-\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](images/e2/e2d4eadef6206fee8006028477c30337e2019000c33ed971f1f048294ef72615.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0590ee5b-b1c4-50b4-ad4a-94aa0c689fb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088045 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fc78834-5b61-5384-b707-af7562e6a703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088455 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088568 #edb/ref 1061 19217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ae790e0-18d0-5862-bc48-9e27aa122785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088569 #edb/ref 1061 19218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b99cc3d5-5767-5c92-9e42-3ff0cb1f004f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088570 #edb/ref 1061 19219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f734cbc-1a62-5061-b22c-9049fa013aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088574 #edb/ref 1061 19224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b1dcbca-d09a-5fe4-992a-eae6e93b1a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088575 #edb/ref 1061 19227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "248bc4f4-7b1d-5e3d-a1d2-9254f6321d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088576 #edb/ref 1061 19229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d77cb04-2793-57de-8295-d5d5ed5ec03a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088583 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088583 #edb/ref 1061 19443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a35e05f-7d8b-5119-a490-40ffe2a5513d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088584 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088584 #edb/ref 1061 19444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d70d9e6-42fa-5815-b0c7-6f8c1a7c0486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088584 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088910 #edb/ref 1064 "When an ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis, we must have $y = 0.$ So, to find the $x$-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$\n\nFor example, let's find the $x$-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + y^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=0$ into the equation and solving the resulting equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(1,0)$ and $(5,0).$ We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/8c/8c14d3e9c1738c41bf97a8e715839f53fd073ac0a502adf0dec594d6e27ef2a0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088910 #edb/ref 1065 3410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088910 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b8c37af-7386-5b3e-9594-49f3b34d1830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088910 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088912 #edb/ref 1064 "When an ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis, we must have ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg) So, to find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of an ellipse, we substitute ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for ![](images/33/3337bbda5af4a3203ef0f6135b36de4f06c1915c4a3ff1bd5358326627c1eecd.svg)\n\nFor example, let's find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercepts of the ellipse given by the equation\n\n![](images/56/5683b5e36c95c36af104a43f9925d36494600eb5800641dd6fee797a0e823cd1.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/51/51a24269e3f466a9c7b886bcab70a365576b28534e323a55aec8b3ebe8d14571.svg) in the equation of the ellipse and solving the resulting equation for ![](images/f4/f4e3bd65ea5e543add33f4a03c10e6b51bba71ff45e1eee33a9ce7e01d064ac7.svg) we get\n![](images/f2/f2d9dd16a35b6be9f8f531de23c6c260904760a2ab07fca95ac5bdd2bd8da448.svg)\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at the points ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) and ![](images/89/890e85845685b97022a3d948405b315a6dcb34195a3a3c7e057c18c22327276f.svg) We can verify this by plotting the graph:\n\n![](images/bf/bf692dc0a62f43c014cd0dd9a704a624e5216f1d53bf8458679cfeab052394e1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088912 #edb/ref 1065 3412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088912 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "24adbc44-0145-5b14-a9b5-a586b757814a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088912 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088918 #edb/ref 1064 "Some ellipses have no $x$-intercepts. For example, consider the ellipse given by the equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} + (y-2)^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, this ellipse does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/f1/f112a9054947dfd03d5b4d6c370fa56e651ea29779480c8cc40e592c89fd6823.png)\n\nAlgebraically, we can tell that an ellipse has no $x$-intercepts if there are no solutions when we substitute $y=0$ into the equation.\n\nFor example, substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse given above, we find no solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+(0-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =-3 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-3)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $x$ such that $(x-3)^2 = -12.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088918 #edb/ref 1065 3435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088918 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f5dc1af-29bc-5b89-9d88-afd2a4ea52c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088918 #edb/ref 1067 "Ellipses with No x-Intercepts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088919 #edb/ref 1064 "Again, some ellipses have no $y$-intercepts. For example, consider the ellipse given by the equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, this ellipse does not intercept the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/64/64e23ef486d53706055b78e2bcd4746d735c9fcf18c5462cf9da5718100574bf.png)\n\nAlgebraically, we can tell that an ellipse has no $y$-intercepts if there are no solutions when we substitute $x=0$ into the equation.\n\nFor example, substituting $x=0$ into the equation of the ellipse given above, we find no solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 4+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{4} & =-3 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^2 = -12.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088919 #edb/ref 1065 3436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088919 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "01941859-2f9f-5be9-adf7-d10e754dd7e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088919 #edb/ref 1067 "Ellipses with No y-Intercepts" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281688 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\pm 3,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0038c158-55e4-54f1-88ca-383efd36482d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281755 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281755 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281755 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0764f3b7-2620-505b-81bc-62a203c31a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281876 #edb/ref 1